-- ID 1119: Special Purpose Frameworks — Analyzing acceptability of modifications to cash basis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont Construction prepares its financial statements on the modified cash basis. The controller records depreciation on equipment and capitalizes long-term assets. A new staff accountant proposes four additional modifications: (1) accruing accounts receivable for work billed but not yet collected, (2) accruing accounts payable for materials received but not yet paid, (3) recording income tax liabilities, and (4) recording prepaid insurance. If the controller adopts all four proposed modifications, how would this affect the classification of the financial statements under AU-C 800, and why?',
  choices = '["All four modifications are acceptable because they have substantial support in accounting literature and would improve the usefulness of the statements without changing the framework classification", "Adding modifications 1, 2, and 4 (receivables, payables, and prepaids) would make the statements indistinguishable from GAAP, disqualifying them as a modified cash basis SPF — while modification 3 (income taxes) is acceptable because it involves a long-term, non-operating item with substantial support", "All four modifications are prohibited under the modified cash basis because only depreciation is ever an acceptable modification", "Adding any of the four modifications would automatically convert the statements to a tax basis framework because tax-basis reporting requires accruals"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under AU-C 800 and AICPA guidance, acceptable modifications to the cash basis must have substantial support and should not make the statements essentially equivalent to GAAP accrual statements. Recording depreciation (already in place) and income tax accruals involve long-term, non-operating items — these have substantial support. However, accruing receivables, payables, and prepaids are routine operating accruals that form the core of accrual accounting. Adding all three would effectively convert the statements to full accrual GAAP, defeating the purpose of the SPF. The critical analytical distinction is between modifications that adjust for long-term items (acceptable) and modifications that replicate the day-to-day accrual mechanics of GAAP (unacceptable). Choice A is incorrect because the acceptability threshold is not just "substantial support" but also whether the modifications collectively destroy the framework''s distinct identity. Choice C is incorrect because multiple modifications are permitted as long as they remain within acceptable bounds. Choice D is incorrect because accruals do not convert statements to tax basis — that framework follows IRC provisions.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1119;

-- ID 1124: Special Purpose Frameworks — Analyzing when modifications cross the line into GAAP
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornfield Medical Group currently prepares modified cash basis financial statements that record depreciation on fixed assets and capitalize long-term debt. The practice administrator wants to add three more modifications: accruing all trade receivables, recording all accounts payable, and accruing prepaid malpractice insurance premiums. The external CPA reviews the proposed modifications. Compare how each modification individually affects the SPF classification and determine which combination, if any, would disqualify the framework.',
  choices = '["Each modification individually is acceptable because modifications are evaluated one at a time, not in combination — the CPA should approve all three", "Accruing trade receivables alone would disqualify the framework because receivables are the defining difference between cash and accrual accounting", "The combination of accruing both trade receivables and trade payables would make the statements substantially similar to GAAP and disqualify the modified cash basis classification — accruing prepaid malpractice insurance alone might be acceptable as a long-term insurance item, but layering routine operating accruals converts the core measurement focus from cash to accrual", "All three modifications are prohibited because the modified cash basis permits only depreciation as a modification, and any other adjustments invalidate the framework"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): The AICPA guidance on modified cash basis evaluates modifications both individually and in the aggregate. Individually, accruing trade receivables converts revenue recognition from cash receipt to the accrual point — a fundamental shift. Accruing trade payables converts expense recognition from cash disbursement to obligation incurrence. Together, these two modifications replicate the core mechanics of accrual accounting for operating transactions. Prepaid malpractice insurance has characteristics of a long-term, non-operating item (multi-year policies) that might qualify individually. However, the critical analysis is the cumulative effect: when modifications collectively make statements indistinguishable from GAAP, the framework loses its identity as an SPF. The engagement CPA must evaluate the aggregate impact, not just each modification in isolation. Choice A is incorrect because AICPA guidance requires evaluating the overall effect of all modifications combined. Choice B is incorrect because a single modification does not automatically disqualify the framework — it depends on context and cumulative impact. Choice D is incorrect because multiple acceptable modifications exist beyond depreciation (e.g., income tax accruals, long-term debt).',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 1124;

-- ID 2354: Special Purpose Frameworks — Effect of framework choice on asset reporting
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Prescott Engineering purchased equipment for $500,000 on January 1, Year 1. Under GAAP, the equipment is depreciated straight-line over 10 years with no residual value. Under the income tax basis, the company elected MACRS 5-year depreciation (Year 1 rate: 20%, Year 2: 32%, Year 3: 19.2%). At the end of Year 2, the tax-basis net book value differs significantly from the GAAP net book value. Analyze how the choice of reporting framework affects the equipment''s balance sheet presentation and what this difference reveals about each framework''s measurement objective.',
  choices = '["The GAAP basis reports equipment at $400,000 ($500,000 less two years of $50,000 depreciation), while the tax basis reports it at $240,000 ($500,000 less $100,000 and $160,000 MACRS) — the $160,000 difference reveals that GAAP aims to match the cost allocation to the asset''s economic consumption pattern, while the tax basis follows IRC provisions designed to accelerate cost recovery as a fiscal policy incentive, not to reflect economic reality", "Both frameworks report the same net book value because depreciation methods are standardized across all reporting bases", "The tax basis always produces a higher net book value because MACRS uses slower depreciation rates than straight-line", "The difference between the two frameworks is immaterial and requires no analysis because both use the same measurement date"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under GAAP, straight-line depreciation allocates $50,000 per year ($500,000 / 10), producing a Year 2 net book value of $400,000. Under the income tax basis using MACRS 5-year, Year 1 depreciation is $100,000 (20%) and Year 2 is $160,000 (32%), totaling $260,000 — producing a net book value of $240,000. The $160,000 difference illustrates the fundamental distinction between the frameworks'' measurement objectives: GAAP depreciation under ASC 360 aims to systematically allocate cost over the estimated useful life, reflecting economic consumption. Tax depreciation under IRC Section 168 accelerates cost recovery to incentivize capital investment — a fiscal policy objective unrelated to the asset''s actual wear. Financial statement users must understand this distinction to avoid comparing tax-basis and GAAP-basis balance sheets as though they measure the same thing. Choice B is incorrect because different frameworks explicitly use different depreciation methods. Choice C reverses the relationship — MACRS produces lower net book values due to acceleration. Choice D is incorrect because a $160,000 difference on a $500,000 asset (32%) is analytically significant.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 2354;

-- ID 2371: Special Purpose Frameworks — Contractual EBITDA vs standard EBITDA analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Bellwood Manufacturing has a loan agreement requiring quarterly financial statements on a contractual basis. The agreement defines EBITDA by excluding restructuring charges, litigation settlements, and asset impairments. In Q3 2026, Bellwood incurred $800,000 in restructuring charges and a $400,000 litigation settlement. Standard EBITDA for Q3 is $3,200,000. The CFO calculates contractual EBITDA at $4,400,000 — $1,200,000 higher than the standard figure. The bank''s covenant requires contractual EBITDA above $4,000,000. Analyze the implications of the $1,200,000 divergence between standard and contractual EBITDA for covenant compliance and the bank''s risk assessment.',
  choices = '["The $1,200,000 divergence is irrelevant because the contractual definition governs and the covenant is satisfied — the bank has no reason to investigate further", "The divergence indicates that Bellwood manipulated its financial results because excluding $1,200,000 of real expenses inflates EBITDA and constitutes financial fraud", "The divergence signals that the covenant provides a cushion the bank may not have intended — Bellwood passes the $4,000,000 threshold only by excluding $1,200,000 of actual charges, meaning the company would fail the covenant under standard EBITDA, and the bank should analyze whether the excluded items are truly nonrecurring or represent a pattern of recurring operational problems that the contractual definition masks", "The bank should require Bellwood to switch from contractual basis to GAAP reporting to eliminate the divergence and provide a more accurate picture"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under the contractual basis framework (AU-C 800), the agreed-upon definitions in the loan agreement govern the financial statement preparation, so Bellwood correctly calculates contractual EBITDA at $4,400,000. However, the analytical insight is critical: the company passes the $4,000,000 covenant solely because $1,200,000 of real expenses are excluded. Under standard EBITDA ($3,200,000), the covenant would be violated. The bank should analyze whether the restructuring charges and litigation settlement are genuinely nonrecurring or whether they represent ongoing operational challenges. If Bellwood regularly incurs "nonrecurring" charges, the contractual EBITDA definition may systematically overstate the company''s debt service capacity — a classic analytical red flag in credit risk assessment. Choice A is incorrect because covenant compliance does not eliminate the bank''s need for analytical judgment about credit risk. Choice B is incorrect because using the contractual definition is not fraud — it follows the agreed-upon terms. Choice D is incorrect because switching frameworks mid-agreement requires renegotiation; the immediate need is analytical scrutiny, not framework change.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 2371;

-- ============================================================
-- STATEMENT OF CASH FLOWS (3 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 1878: Statement of Cash Flows — Depreciation add-back mechanics and why noncash items have differential directions
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Waverly Corp. reports net income of $320,000 for Year 1. The indirect method reconciliation includes three adjustments: depreciation expense of $45,000, a gain on sale of equipment of $18,000, and amortization of a bond discount of $6,000. A junior analyst questions why depreciation and discount amortization are both noncash items yet move in the same direction (both added back), while the gain on sale is subtracted. Analyze why each adjustment moves in its specific direction and what the adjusted operating cash flow reveals about the relationship between accrual income and cash generation.',
  choices = '["All three items should be added to net income because they are all noncash items — the gain on sale was incorrectly subtracted by the controller", "Depreciation ($45,000) is added because it reduced income without using cash; the bond discount amortization ($6,000) is added because it increased interest expense above cash interest paid; and the gain on sale ($18,000) is subtracted because it inflated operating income but the cash proceeds belong in investing activities — operating cash flow of $353,000 exceeds net income by $33,000, indicating that accrual adjustments (primarily depreciation) generate a cash flow cushion above reported earnings", "The gain is subtracted because it represents a loss, and depreciation is added because it represents income — the adjustments correct classification errors in the income statement", "All noncash items cancel out in the indirect method, so operating cash flow always equals net income regardless of adjustments"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 230, the indirect method adjusts net income by reversing noncash items that distorted the accrual-to-cash conversion. Depreciation ($45,000) is a noncash expense that reduced net income but required no cash — adding it back corrects for the understatement of cash. Bond discount amortization ($6,000) increased interest expense above the cash coupon payment (the company paid less cash interest than the expense recorded) — adding it back corrects for the overstatement of expense relative to cash outflow. The gain on sale ($18,000) increased net income but the actual cash proceeds are classified as investing activities — subtracting it removes the operating income inflation and prevents double-counting when the cash appears in investing. Operating cash flow = $320,000 + $45,000 - $18,000 + $6,000 = $353,000. (A) is wrong because gains must be removed from operating to avoid double-counting with investing. (C) mischaracterizes the purpose of the adjustments — they correct the accrual-to-cash conversion, not classification errors. (D) is wrong because noncash items routinely cause operating cash flow to differ from net income.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1878;

-- ID 1886: Statement of Cash Flows — Direct method supplier payment analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Greystone Distributors reports cost of goods sold of $2,400,000 for Year 2. Beginning inventory was $310,000, ending inventory was $425,000, beginning accounts payable was $180,000, and ending accounts payable was $145,000. In Year 1, COGS was $2,200,000 with inventory stable and accounts payable increasing by $30,000. The CFO notices that cash paid to suppliers in Year 2 significantly exceeds Year 1 despite only a modest COGS increase. Analyze why cash paid to suppliers diverged from the COGS trend and what this indicates about the company''s cash management.',
  choices = '["Cash paid to suppliers is lower in Year 2 because higher ending inventory means the company deferred payments to build stock, preserving cash", "Cash paid to suppliers equals COGS in both years because inventory and payable changes do not affect cash payments", "The divergence is caused solely by the COGS increase and the working capital changes are immaterial adjustments that can be ignored", "Cash paid to suppliers in Year 2 is $2,550,000 ($2,400,000 COGS + $115,000 inventory increase + $35,000 AP decrease), compared to approximately $2,170,000 in Year 1 — the $380,000 increase reflects a double cash drain from both inventory accumulation (investing in stock) and accelerated vendor payments (reducing payables), compressing operating cash flow even though the underlying COGS increase was only $200,000"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Under the direct method (ASC 230), cash paid to suppliers is calculated as: Purchases + Decrease in AP (or - Increase in AP). Purchases = COGS + Ending Inventory - Beginning Inventory = $2,400,000 + $425,000 - $310,000 = $2,515,000. Cash paid = $2,515,000 + ($180,000 - $145,000) = $2,515,000 + $35,000 = $2,550,000. In Year 1, with stable inventory and AP increasing $30,000: Cash paid = $2,200,000 - $30,000 = $2,170,000. The $380,000 year-over-year increase in cash payments (vs. only $200,000 COGS increase) reveals two compounding cash drains: the company built $115,000 of additional inventory (requiring cash outlay above COGS) and simultaneously reduced payables by $35,000 (paying suppliers faster). This pattern — growing inventory plus shrinking payables — aggressively consumes cash and warrants investigation into whether inventory is building due to anticipated demand or slowing sales. Choice A reverses the logic — higher inventory requires more cash, not less. Choice B is incorrect because working capital changes are the core of cash flow analysis. Choice C is incorrect because the $180,000 working capital impact exceeds the $200,000 COGS increase.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 1886;

-- ID 1889: Statement of Cash Flows — Bond premium amortization effect on operating cash flow
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Meridian Corp. reports net income of $180,000. The controller''s indirect method reconciliation includes depreciation of $25,000, amortization of a bond premium of $3,000, and a decrease in inventory of $12,000. The audit intern questions why the $3,000 bond premium amortization is subtracted from net income while the $25,000 depreciation is added. Both are noncash items. Analyze why these two noncash adjustments move in opposite directions and what each reveals about the relationship between accrual income and cash flows.',
  choices = '["Both should be added to net income because all noncash items increase cash flow — the controller made an error by subtracting the bond premium amortization", "The bond premium amortization is subtracted because it represents a financing activity that must be reclassified from operating to financing", "Depreciation is added because it reduced net income without using cash (expense overstates cash outflow), while bond premium amortization is subtracted because it reduced interest expense below the actual cash interest paid — the premium amortization caused net income to overstate operating cash flow by $3,000 because the company paid more cash interest than the income statement reflects", "Both items should be subtracted because noncash adjustments always reduce cash from operations under the indirect method"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under ASC 230, the indirect method adjusts net income to operating cash flow by reversing noncash items that distorted the accrual-to-cash relationship. Depreciation ($25,000) is a noncash expense that reduced net income but required no cash outlay — adding it back corrects the understatement. Bond premium amortization works differently: when a bond is issued at a premium, the periodic amortization reduces interest expense below the cash coupon payment. For example, if the coupon is $15,000 but interest expense is $12,000 (after $3,000 premium amortization), net income was $3,000 higher than the cash outflow warrants. Subtracting $3,000 corrects for this overstatement. The key insight is directionality: depreciation understates cash flow (subtract from income but no cash used), while premium amortization overstates cash flow (income higher than cash outflow). Total operating cash flow = $180,000 + $25,000 - $3,000 + $12,000 = $214,000. Choice A is incorrect because noncash items can either overstate or understate cash flow depending on their nature. Choice B is incorrect because premium amortization is an operating adjustment, not a reclassification. Choice D is incorrect because the direction depends on whether the item understates or overstates cash.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 1889;

-- ============================================================
-- CONTINGENCIES (3 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 1140: Contingencies — Purchase commitment loss recognition vs inventory write-down comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ashford Manufacturing has two inventory-related situations at December 31, Year 1. First, a noncancelable purchase commitment to buy 5,000 units of raw material at $80 per unit when the current market price has declined to $65 per unit. Second, 3,000 units of finished goods inventory carried at cost of $95 per unit with a net realizable value of $78 per unit. Compare the loss recognition timing and measurement for these two situations and explain why ASC 450 and ASC 330 require different approaches.',
  choices = '["Both situations require identical treatment — recognize a loss for the full market decline and reduce the carrying amount to market value", "The purchase commitment loss of $75,000 is recognized immediately under ASC 450 because the contract creates a present obligation at above-market prices, while the inventory write-down of $51,000 is recognized under ASC 330''s lower of cost or NRV rule — both require current-period loss recognition, but for different conceptual reasons: the commitment loss arises from a contractual obligation the company cannot escape, while the inventory write-down arises from the principle that assets should not be carried above recoverable amounts", "Only the inventory write-down is recognized because purchase commitments are off-balance-sheet items that require disclosure only, regardless of the price decline", "Only the purchase commitment loss is recognized because inventory is not written down until it is actually sold below cost"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The purchase commitment loss is ($80 - $65) x 5,000 = $75,000, recognized under ASC 450 as a loss on purchase commitment with a corresponding accrued liability. The commitment creates a binding obligation to purchase at above-market prices — the loss is probable and estimable. The inventory write-down is ($95 - $78) x 3,000 = $51,000, recognized under ASC 330-10-35 (lower of cost or net realizable value). Although both result in current-period loss recognition, the conceptual foundations differ: ASC 450 addresses losses from existing commitments where future performance is unfavorable, while ASC 330 addresses the carrying value of assets already on the balance sheet. This distinction matters because the commitment loss creates a new liability (accrued loss), while the inventory write-down reduces an existing asset. Choice A is incorrect because the treatment differs in the accounts affected and the authoritative basis. Choice C is incorrect because noncancelable purchase commitments with other-than-temporary declines require accrual, not just disclosure. Choice D is incorrect because inventory is written down when NRV falls below cost, not deferred until sale.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1140;

-- ID 2149: Contingencies — Joint-and-several liability analysis with changing co-defendant solvency
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Falcon Corp., Delta Inc., and Summit LLC are jointly and severally liable for a $9,000,000 environmental remediation judgment. At December 31, Year 1, all three defendants are financially viable, and Falcon accrues its proportionate share of $3,000,000. During Year 2, Delta Inc. files for bankruptcy and its assets are insufficient to cover any remediation costs. Summit LLC remains solvent. Analyze how Delta''s bankruptcy changes Falcon''s loss accrual and what the joint-and-several liability doctrine implies for Falcon''s maximum exposure.',
  choices = '["Falcon''s accrual remains at $3,000,000 because the original proportionate allocation is fixed at the judgment date and cannot change based on co-defendant solvency", "Falcon should reduce its accrual to zero because Delta''s bankruptcy triggers an automatic stay that suspends all co-defendants'' obligations", "Falcon should increase its accrual to the full $9,000,000 because joint-and-several liability means each defendant is individually liable for the entire amount regardless of co-defendant solvency", "Falcon should increase its accrual from $3,000,000 to $4,500,000, splitting Delta''s $3,000,000 share equally with Summit — under joint-and-several liability, each remaining solvent defendant bears a proportionate share of the insolvent party''s obligation, and Falcon must reassess its probable exposure whenever co-defendant solvency changes materially"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Under ASC 450, the accrual for a loss contingency must reflect the entity''s best estimate of probable loss. Joint-and-several liability means each defendant can be held liable for the entire $9,000,000, but when co-defendants are solvent, Falcon reasonably estimates its proportionate share. Delta''s bankruptcy eliminates $3,000,000 of co-defendant capacity — the plaintiff can now pursue only Falcon and Summit for Delta''s share. The most likely outcome is that Falcon and Summit each bear half of Delta''s $3,000,000 obligation, increasing Falcon''s probable exposure to $4,500,000. While Falcon''s theoretical maximum exposure is $9,000,000 (if Summit also became insolvent), the current best estimate with one remaining solvent co-defendant is $4,500,000. ASC 450-20-25 requires updating estimates when new information — like a co-defendant''s insolvency — changes the probable loss amount. Choice A is incorrect because contingency estimates must be updated for changed circumstances. Choice B is incorrect because Delta''s bankruptcy stay does not extinguish other defendants'' joint obligations. Choice C overstates the exposure — while $9,000,000 is theoretically possible, the best estimate considers Summit''s continuing solvency.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 2149;

-- ID 2152: Contingencies — Superfund PRP allocation analysis with contribution uncertainty
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Redstone Inc. is notified by the EPA that it is one of twelve potentially responsible parties (PRPs) at a contaminated Superfund site. Total remediation costs are estimated at $10,000,000. The EPA allocates Redstone a 15% share based on waste volume contributed. However, three of the twelve PRPs are defunct companies with no assets, representing a combined 25% allocation. The remaining eight solvent PRPs are negotiating cost-sharing. Analyze how the defunct PRPs'' insolvency affects Redstone''s accrual and what factors Redstone should consider when estimating its probable loss.',
  choices = '["Redstone should accrue only its 15% allocated share ($1,500,000) because the EPA allocation is final and binding regardless of co-PRP solvency", "Redstone should accrue its 15% share plus a proportionate share of the orphan allocation — the defunct PRPs'' combined 25% ($2,500,000) will be redistributed among the eight solvent PRPs, increasing Redstone''s probable exposure beyond the initial 15% allocation to approximately $1,917,000, calculated as $1,500,000 base share plus $416,667 orphan share ($2,500,000 x 15%/75% solvent allocation)", "Redstone should accrue the full $10,000,000 because CERCLA joint-and-several liability makes each PRP responsible for all remediation costs", "Redstone should accrue zero because the presence of twelve PRPs means the individual share is too uncertain to estimate"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under CERCLA and ASC 450 environmental guidance (ASC 410-30), Redstone must estimate its share of remediation costs considering all available information, including co-PRP solvency. The initial 15% EPA allocation ($1,500,000) is a starting point, but the three defunct PRPs create "orphan shares" totaling 25% ($2,500,000) that must be absorbed by solvent parties. Redstone''s proportionate share of the orphan amount is calculated as its share of the solvent allocation: 15%/75% x $2,500,000 = $500,000, bringing total estimated exposure to approximately $2,000,000 (or $1,917,000 using the $416,667 calculation shown). ASC 450 requires accrual at the best estimate when the loss is probable and estimable — ignoring orphan shares would understate the probable liability. Factors Redstone should also consider include: pending contribution actions against defunct PRPs'' insurers, state cost-recovery programs, and whether other solvent PRPs may become insolvent during the remediation period. Choice A is incorrect because EPA allocations are preliminary and do not account for actual collection risk. Choice C overstates exposure — while CERCLA imposes joint-and-several liability, the best estimate considers other solvent PRPs'' willingness and ability to pay. Choice D is incorrect because the EPA allocation provides a reasonable basis for estimation.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 2152;

