-- ACCOUNTING CHANGES AND ERROR CORRECTIONS (3 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 5253: Accounting Changes — Retrospective restatement vs prospective treatment comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Whitfield Industries discovers two issues while preparing its Year 3 financial statements. First, the company failed to record $50,000 of depreciation expense in Year 1 (an error). Second, the company revised the useful life of its manufacturing equipment from 10 years to 7 years beginning in Year 3 (a change in estimate). The controller must apply different treatments to each item. Compare the financial statement effects of these two items and explain why ASC 250 requires different approaches for errors versus changes in estimate.',
  choices = '["Both items should be restated retrospectively because they both relate to depreciation and ASC 250 treats all depreciation-related adjustments identically", "Both items should be applied prospectively because they both involve uncertainty in asset life estimates", "The error should be corrected prospectively by recording $50,000 of additional depreciation in Year 3, while the estimate change requires restating all prior periods to reflect the 7-year life", "The error requires restating Year 1 financial statements (if presented comparatively) and adjusting beginning retained earnings of the earliest period shown, because users relied on misstated information — the estimate change is applied prospectively from Year 3 because it reflects new information about remaining useful life that was not available earlier, and retroactive adjustment would inappropriately penalize prior-period decisions made with the best information then available"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): ASC 250 distinguishes errors from estimate changes based on the nature of the underlying issue. The omitted $50,000 depreciation in Year 1 was an error — the information existed to record it correctly, and the failure produced materially misstated financial statements that users relied upon. Restatement corrects the historical record: Year 1 depreciation expense increases by $50,000, net income decreases, and accumulated depreciation is adjusted. If Year 1 is not presented, the cumulative effect adjusts opening retained earnings per ASC 250-10-45-23. The useful life change from 10 to 7 years is a change in estimate under ASC 250-10-45-17 — it reflects updated judgment about the asset''s remaining service potential. Prospective treatment is appropriate because the original 10-year estimate was reasonable when made; it is not an error. The remaining book value is depreciated over the revised remaining life starting in Year 3. Choice A is incorrect because errors and estimate changes receive fundamentally different treatments. Choice B is incorrect because errors require restatement, not prospective correction. Choice C reverses the required treatments.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 5253;

-- ID 5255: Accounting Changes — Depreciation method change classification analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont Manufacturing changes its depreciation method from double-declining balance (DDB) to straight-line for all factory equipment beginning January 1, Year 3. The controller argues this is a change in accounting principle requiring retrospective application, like a change from FIFO to LIFO inventory. The audit manager disagrees and instructs the controller to apply the change prospectively. Analyze why ASC 250 classifies a depreciation method change differently from an inventory method change, even though both involve switching between acceptable GAAP methods.',
  choices = '["The audit manager is wrong — both changes are accounting principle changes requiring retrospective application because they involve switching from one acceptable GAAP method to another", "ASC 250 classifies the depreciation method change as a change in estimate effected by a change in principle — the depreciation method reflects management''s judgment about the pattern of economic benefit consumption, which is inherently an estimate, so prospective treatment is appropriate; by contrast, an inventory method change (FIFO to LIFO) is a pure principle change because it involves selecting a different cost-flow assumption that does not depend on estimating the physical consumption of assets", "The depreciation method change should be applied retrospectively while the inventory change should be applied prospectively, because depreciation affects the balance sheet while inventory affects the income statement", "Both should be applied prospectively because ASC 250 requires prospective treatment for all voluntary accounting changes"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): ASC 250-10-45-18 specifically classifies a change in depreciation method as a "change in accounting estimate effected by a change in accounting principle." The rationale is that depreciation method selection is inseparable from management''s estimate of how the asset''s economic benefits are consumed over time — switching from DDB to straight-line reflects a revised assessment that benefits are consumed evenly rather than in a declining pattern. Because the method embodies an estimate, ASC 250 requires prospective treatment: the remaining undepreciated balance is allocated over the remaining useful life using the new method. An inventory cost-flow assumption change (e.g., FIFO to LIFO under ASC 330) is a pure principle change — it does not reflect an estimate about physical consumption but rather a choice between acceptable cost-assignment methods. Pure principle changes require retrospective application under ASC 250-10-45-5. This dual classification is a frequently tested distinction. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 250 explicitly treats these two changes differently. Choice C reverses the treatments. Choice D is incorrect because principle changes generally require retrospective application.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 5255;

-- ID 5259: Accounting Changes — Revenue method change retrospective application analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Cornerstone Construction changes from the completed-contract method to the percentage-of-completion method for long-term contracts beginning in Year 3. The company has two active contracts: Contract A (started Year 1, 60% complete at end of Year 2, to finish Year 4) and Contract B (started Year 2, 30% complete at end of Year 2, to finish Year 3). Under completed-contract, zero revenue was recognized for these contracts through Year 2. Analyze the effect of retrospective application on the Year 2 comparative financial statements and the opening retained earnings adjustment.',
  choices = '["No retrospective adjustment is needed because the contracts are still in progress — retrospective application only affects completed contracts", "Retrospective application requires restating Year 2 to recognize cumulative revenue and costs for both contracts through Year 2 — Contract A would show revenue based on 60% completion and Contract B based on 30% completion, with corresponding cost recognition, as though percentage-of-completion had always been used; the opening retained earnings for Year 2 would also be adjusted for Contract A''s Year 1 activity (the cumulative effect on the period not presented), producing financial statements that accurately depict what results would have been under the new method from inception", "Only current-year contracts should be adjusted because ASC 250 prohibits restating prior-period long-term contracts due to the impracticability exception", "The change should be applied prospectively for construction contracts because construction accounting is specifically exempted from retrospective application under ASC 606"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 250-10-45-5, a change in accounting principle is applied retrospectively by adjusting each prior period presented as if the new method had always been used. For Contract A (started Year 1): Year 1 activity is not directly presented in the Year 3 comparative statements, so its cumulative effect (revenue and costs through Year 1 under percentage-of-completion) adjusts the opening retained earnings of the earliest period presented (Year 2). Year 2 is restated to show Contract A''s incremental progress from the end of Year 1 to Year 2 (e.g., moving from, say, 35% to 60% complete). Contract B (started Year 2): Year 2 is restated to show 30% completion revenue and costs that were previously deferred under completed-contract. The result is that comparative Year 2 financial statements look as though the company had always used percentage-of-completion. Choice A is incorrect because retrospective application applies to all affected contracts, regardless of completion status. Choice C is incorrect because the impracticability exception applies only when period-specific effects cannot be determined — here, completion percentages provide the necessary data. Choice D is incorrect because no such exemption exists; ASC 606 governs revenue recognition for new contracts but does not override ASC 250''s retrospective application requirement for principle changes.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 5259;

-- ============================================================
-- GOVERNMENTAL ACCOUNTING FUNDAMENTALS (3 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 5275: Governmental Accounting — Encumbrance accounting effect analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Cedar Valley School District issues a $500,000 purchase order for classroom technology on October 15. By fiscal year-end (June 30), only $350,000 of the equipment has been delivered and invoiced. The remaining $150,000 is expected to arrive in August. The finance director records encumbrances at the purchase order date and reverses them upon delivery. Analyze the effect of the encumbrance system on the fund balance classifications at June 30 and compare how the $150,000 outstanding commitment affects fund-level reporting versus government-wide reporting.',
  choices = '["The outstanding $150,000 encumbrance reduces available fund balance by being reported as a committed or assigned component of fund balance under GASB 54, signaling to the governing board that these resources are earmarked for existing obligations — at the government-wide level, the encumbrance has no effect because the purchase order creates neither an asset nor a liability under full accrual accounting until the goods are received, illustrating a fundamental difference between budgetary fund accounting and economic resource reporting", "The $150,000 encumbrance is reported as an expenditure in the governmental fund because the commitment is binding, and as an expense in the government-wide statements", "Encumbrances are not reported in financial statements under any circumstances — they are internal management controls that exist only in the general ledger", "The $150,000 should be reported as a current liability in both fund-level and government-wide statements because the purchase order creates a legally binding obligation"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under GASB 54, outstanding encumbrances at year-end are reported as part of the fund balance — typically as committed (if approved by the governing board) or assigned (if designated by a lower authority). The $150,000 outstanding purchase order reduces the amount available for new appropriations, providing budgetary control. At the fund level, the encumbrance entry (Dr. Encumbrances $500,000, Cr. Budgetary Fund Balance Reserved for Encumbrances $500,000) was partially reversed when $350,000 was delivered and recorded as an expenditure. The remaining $150,000 encumbrance stays open, restricting fund balance. However, in the government-wide statements (which use full accrual under GASB 34), encumbrances are not recognized because a purchase order alone does not create a liability or transfer economic resources — no asset has been received and no obligation has been incurred. This dual reporting demonstrates why governmental fund statements and government-wide statements serve different purposes. Choice B is incorrect because encumbrances are not expenditures — expenditures are recorded upon delivery. Choice C is incorrect because GASB requires disclosure of encumbrances through fund balance classification. Choice D is incorrect because a purchase order is an executory contract, not a liability.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 5275;

-- ID 5278: Governmental Accounting — Budgetary entry surplus vs deficit analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Pinecrest County adopts its annual budget with estimated revenues of $12,000,000 and appropriations of $11,200,000. The budget entry records: Dr. Estimated Revenues $12,000,000, Cr. Appropriations $11,200,000, Cr. Budgetary Fund Balance $800,000. At year-end, actual revenues are $11,600,000 and actual expenditures are $11,400,000. Analyze the difference between the budgeted surplus ($800,000) and the actual surplus ($200,000) and explain what this variance reveals about the county''s fiscal management.',
  choices = '["The $600,000 variance ($800,000 budgeted surplus minus $200,000 actual surplus) indicates fiscal mismanagement because the county failed to achieve its revenue targets and overspent its appropriations", "The actual surplus of $200,000 is satisfactory because revenues exceeded expenditures, making the budgetary comparison irrelevant", "The budgetary-to-actual comparison reveals two offsetting variances: revenues came in $400,000 under estimate ($11,600,000 vs. $12,000,000), an unfavorable variance suggesting overly optimistic projections or economic softness — expenditures exceeded the original appropriation by $200,000 ($11,400,000 vs. $11,200,000), an unfavorable variance suggesting inadequate cost controls or supplemental appropriations — the combined $600,000 deterioration from the planned surplus is analytically significant because it indicates both revenue estimation and expenditure control weaknesses", "The variances cannot be analyzed because budgetary entries use estimated amounts that have no connection to actual operating results"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under GASB 34, governmental funds present a budgetary comparison schedule (or statement) that juxtaposes original budget, final budget, and actual results. The revenue shortfall of $400,000 ($12,000,000 - $11,600,000) is an unfavorable variance that could indicate over-optimistic revenue projections, delayed property tax collections, or an economic downturn reducing sales tax receipts. The expenditure overage of $200,000 ($11,400,000 - $11,200,000) is unfavorable and could reflect supplemental appropriations, emergency spending, or inadequate budgetary controls. Together, these reduced the projected $800,000 surplus to an actual $200,000 surplus — a 75% decline. This budgetary analysis is a cornerstone of governmental accountability: citizens and oversight bodies use it to evaluate whether elected officials managed public resources as promised. The closing entry reverses the budgetary accounts and records actual results in the fund balance. Choice A overstates the conclusion — the $200,000 excess of expenditures over appropriation may reflect approved supplemental appropriations, not mismanagement. Choice B is incorrect because budgetary compliance is a fundamental accountability metric in governmental accounting. Choice D is incorrect because GASB requires budgetary-to-actual comparison precisely because the connection is analytically essential.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 5278;

-- ID 5282: Governmental Accounting — Interfund transfer vs expenditure classification analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Lakewood Township''s General Fund provides $400,000 to the Capital Projects Fund for a new fire station. The finance director initially records this as an expenditure in the General Fund. The external auditor reclassifies it as "Other Financing Uses — Transfers Out." Separately, the General Fund pays $50,000 to the Internal Service Fund for vehicle maintenance services. This payment is recorded as an expenditure. Analyze why two transactions involving General Fund outflows to other funds receive different classifications and what each classification reveals about the nature of the transaction.',
  choices = '["Both should be classified as expenditures because all cash outflows from the General Fund reduce available resources identically", "The $400,000 transfer is classified as Other Financing Uses because it is a nonreciprocal resource movement — the General Fund receives nothing in return and the transfer merely reallocates resources between funds for capital purposes — the $50,000 payment to the ISF is an expenditure because it is a reciprocal exchange: the General Fund receives vehicle maintenance services in return, making it economically equivalent to purchasing services from an external vendor", "The $400,000 should be an expenditure because it is larger, while the $50,000 is a transfer because interfund service transactions are always classified as transfers", "Both should be classified as Other Financing Uses because all interfund outflows are transfers under GASB standards"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under GASB Statement No. 34, the classification of interfund transactions depends on their economic substance. Interfund transfers (like the $400,000 to Capital Projects) are nonreciprocal flows — one fund provides resources to another without receiving goods or services in return. They are reported as "Other Financing Sources/Uses" below the revenues-over-expenditures line because they represent resource reallocations, not costs of delivering services. Interfund services used (like the $50,000 to the ISF) are reciprocal exchanges — the General Fund receives vehicle maintenance, which is an operating cost. These are reported as expenditures, identical to how the General Fund would record a payment to an external garage. This distinction matters for analysis: expenditures represent the cost of providing governmental services, while transfers represent policy decisions about resource allocation. Combining them would distort the cost of services in the General Fund. Choice A is incorrect because identical cash impact does not determine classification — economic substance does. Choice C reverses the size-based logic, which is not a GASB criterion. Choice D is incorrect because interfund service transactions are expenditures/revenues, not transfers.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 5282;

-- ============================================================
-- CONCEPTUAL FRAMEWORK AND STANDARDS (3 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 5217: Conceptual Framework — Cost constraint vs materiality as reporting filters
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont Financial Services is preparing its annual financial statements and faces two disclosure decisions. First, the company could provide segment-level cash flow data at a cost of $150,000 in additional audit and reporting work — analysts have indicated this data would be moderately useful for valuation models. Second, the company is considering whether to disclose a $12,000 accounting policy related to office supplies expensing, which management considers trivially small relative to $80,000,000 in total revenues. Analyze how the cost constraint and materiality filter operate differently in determining whether each disclosure should be provided.',
  choices = '["Both decisions are governed by the cost constraint because they both involve management evaluating whether the effort of disclosure is worthwhile", "The segment cash flow decision involves the cost constraint — the company must assess whether the $150,000 cost of producing the information is justified by the decision-usefulness to investors, which is a cost-benefit analysis external to the information itself — the office supplies policy decision involves materiality, which asks whether omitting the information could influence user decisions, and since $12,000 is trivially small relative to total revenues, omission would not affect any reasonable investor''s assessment, so disclosure is not required", "Materiality governs both decisions because any information a company chooses not to disclose is by definition immaterial", "Neither filter applies because the Conceptual Framework requires disclosure of all financial information regardless of cost or size"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The FASB Conceptual Framework (SFAC No. 8) establishes two distinct filters for financial reporting. The cost constraint (QC35-QC39) recognizes that reporting information has costs (preparation, audit, dissemination) that must be justified by the benefits to users — it is a cost-benefit analysis comparing reporting burden to decision-usefulness. For the segment cash flow data, management must weigh whether $150,000 in additional costs is justified by the incremental value to analysts. Materiality (QC11) is an entity-specific relevance threshold — information is material if omitting it could reasonably influence decisions. The $12,000 office supplies policy (0.015% of revenue) falls below any reasonable materiality threshold, making its omission appropriate regardless of cost. The critical distinction: the cost constraint applies even to material information (a material disclosure might not be required if costs vastly exceed benefits), while materiality applies regardless of cost (an immaterial item need not be disclosed even if disclosure costs nothing). Choice A is incorrect because materiality is not a cost-benefit analysis but a significance threshold. Choice C is incorrect because materiality has a specific definition unrelated to management''s disclosure preferences. Choice D is incorrect because both constraints are explicitly part of the framework.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 5217;

-- ID 5219: Conceptual Framework — Analogical guidance hierarchy analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Westfield Biotech enters into a novel transaction — a revenue-sharing arrangement with a university for jointly developed intellectual property — that is not specifically addressed by any topic in the FASB ASC. The controller identifies three potential sources of guidance: (1) ASC 606 for revenue from contracts with customers, which addresses similar multi-party arrangements, (2) an IFRS standard that specifically covers collaborative research arrangements, and (3) a common industry practice used by other biotech companies for similar transactions. Under ASC 105, analyze the order in which these sources should be consulted and why the hierarchy matters for consistent financial reporting.',
  choices = '["The controller should apply IFRS first because it specifically addresses the transaction, and specific guidance always takes precedence over analogical guidance", "All three sources carry equal weight under ASC 105 and the controller may choose whichever produces the most favorable accounting treatment", "The controller should first apply ASC 606 by analogy (guidance within the ASC for similar transactions), then consider IFRS and industry practice as nonauthoritative supplements — the hierarchy prioritizes internal ASC consistency because analogical application of authoritative guidance produces more comparable results than importing guidance from a different framework or relying on unaudited industry conventions", "Industry practice should be applied first because it reflects how similar transactions are actually handled, and practical application supersedes theoretical frameworks"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): ASC 105-10-05-2 establishes a clear hierarchy for transactions not specifically addressed by the Codification. The first step is to consider guidance within the ASC for analogous transactions — here, ASC 606''s principles for identifying performance obligations and allocating transaction prices in multi-element arrangements could be applied by analogy to the university revenue-sharing arrangement. Only if ASC analogical guidance is insufficient should the controller look to nonauthoritative sources, including FASB Concepts Statements, IFRS, AICPA guidance, and industry practice. The hierarchy matters because it promotes consistency: if every company resolved novel transactions by looking first to IFRS or industry practice, U.S. financial statements would lose comparability. Analogical application of the ASC keeps reporting grounded in a single authoritative framework. IFRS (source 2) and industry practice (source 3) can inform the analysis but do not override ASC-based reasoning. Choice A is incorrect because IFRS is not authoritative for U.S. GAAP reporters and does not take precedence over ASC analogies. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 105 establishes a hierarchy, not equal weighting. Choice D is incorrect because industry practice is the least authoritative source in the hierarchy.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 5219;

-- ID 5227: Conceptual Framework — Fair value vs historical cost measurement analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Meridian Holdings owns two assets: (1) a portfolio of publicly traded equity securities purchased for $2,000,000 now worth $3,200,000, reported at fair value under ASC 321, and (2) a manufacturing facility purchased for $5,000,000 with accumulated depreciation of $1,500,000, reported at historical cost less depreciation under ASC 360. The CFO questions why the Conceptual Framework permits different measurement bases for different assets within the same set of financial statements. Analyze how each measurement basis serves the objective of useful financial information and what information is sacrificed by each approach.',
  choices = '["Historical cost is always superior because it provides verifiable, transaction-based amounts — fair value introduces unnecessary volatility and should not be used for any asset", "Fair value for equity securities provides relevance by reflecting current market conditions and exit prices that investors use for valuation — historical cost for the facility provides reliability and verifiability but sacrifices relevance because the $3,500,000 carrying amount may differ significantly from current market value — the framework tolerates this inconsistency because each measurement basis optimizes a different qualitative characteristic depending on the asset''s nature and the availability of reliable market inputs", "The inconsistency violates the Conceptual Framework because the comparability characteristic requires all assets to use the same measurement basis", "Fair value should be used for both assets because relevance always outweighs faithful representation under the framework''s hierarchy"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The FASB Conceptual Framework (SFAC No. 8, Chapter 6) recognizes multiple measurement bases because no single basis optimally serves both relevance and faithful representation for all assets. Equity securities with readily determinable fair values have observable market prices (Level 1 inputs under ASC 820), making fair value both relevant (reflects current economic reality) and reliably measurable. The $1,200,000 unrealized gain provides information investors use to assess current portfolio value. For the manufacturing facility, active markets for used industrial buildings rarely exist, making fair value estimation subjective and costly. Historical cost less depreciation ($3,500,000) provides faithful representation through verifiable, transaction-based measurement, even though it sacrifices some relevance — the facility''s current market value, replacement cost, or value in use may differ materially from carrying amount. The framework accepts this trade-off because forcing fair value measurement where reliable inputs are unavailable would reduce rather than enhance the quality of financial information. Choice A is incorrect because historical cost sacrifices relevance for liquid assets with observable prices. Choice C is incorrect because the framework does not require uniform measurement — it permits tailored approaches. Choice D is incorrect because the framework treats relevance and faithful representation as complementary fundamental characteristics, not hierarchical.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 5227;

-- ============================================================
-- PAYABLES AND ACCRUED LIABILITIES (3 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 5233: Payables — Warranty accrual vs actual claims divergence analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornfield Electronics provides a one-year warranty on products sold. In Year 1, the company sold 5,000 units and estimated warranty costs at $8 per unit, accruing $40,000. Actual warranty claims paid in Year 1 totaled $12,000, leaving a $28,000 warranty liability. In Year 2, the company sold 6,000 units and again estimated $8 per unit ($48,000 accrual). However, Year 2 actual claims against both Year 1 and Year 2 products totaled $62,000. Analyze what the Year 2 claims experience reveals about the adequacy of the $8-per-unit estimate and the effect on the warranty liability balance.',
  choices = '["The $8-per-unit estimate is adequate because total estimated costs over two years ($88,000) are close to total claims ($74,000), and the warranty liability is simply a timing difference", "The $62,000 in claims proves the estimate is too high because the ending liability of $14,000 represents excess reserves that should be reversed", "The Year 2 claims of $62,000 against a combined available liability of $76,000 ($28,000 carryover plus $48,000 new accrual) leaves a Year 2 ending liability of $14,000 — but the actual claim rate exceeding the accrual rate in Year 2 suggests the $8-per-unit estimate may be insufficient, particularly since Year 1 products generated above-expected claims in their second year, indicating potential quality issues that should prompt management to reassess the per-unit estimate upward to avoid future underaccrual", "Warranty estimates cannot be revised once established, so the $8-per-unit rate must continue regardless of actual experience"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under ASC 460 (warranties) and the matching principle, warranty expense is estimated and accrued at the time of sale. The Year 2 ending liability = $28,000 (Year 1 carryover) + $48,000 (Year 2 accrual) - $62,000 (claims paid) = $14,000. The analytical concern is that Year 2 claims ($62,000) exceeded the Year 2 accrual ($48,000) by $14,000, consuming part of the Year 1 carryover. This means the $8-per-unit estimate is not keeping pace with actual experience — particularly for Year 1 units, which appear to have generated more claims in their second warranty year than expected. Management must evaluate under ASC 450 whether the per-unit estimate should be revised upward (a change in accounting estimate under ASC 250, applied prospectively). If the pattern continues, the warranty liability will deplete and the company will face underaccrual. Choice A is incorrect because the trend matters more than cumulative totals — a declining liability balance with accelerating claims is a warning sign. Choice B is incorrect because a $14,000 ending balance is lower than expected, not higher. Choice D is incorrect because warranty estimates are routinely revised based on actual experience under ASC 250.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 5233;

-- ID 5236: Payables — Vesting vs accumulating compensated absences analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Greystone Corp. has two categories of compensated absences for its 200 employees. Category 1: Vacation — employees earn 15 days per year; unused days accumulate up to 30 days and vest upon termination (employees receive cash payment for unused days). Category 2: Personal days — employees receive 3 days per year; unused days accumulate to the next year (up to 6 days maximum) but do not vest (no payment upon termination). At December 31, the average daily pay rate is $250 and total unused vacation days are 1,800 and total unused personal days are 280. Analyze whether ASC 710 requires accrual for each category and explain how vesting versus accumulation affects the measurement.',
  choices = '["Only vacation days should be accrued because personal days do not vest and ASC 710 only requires accrual for vesting compensated absences", "Both categories require accrual under ASC 710 — vacation days ($450,000 = 1,800 x $250) are accrued because they both accumulate and vest, making the obligation certain upon termination; personal days also require accrual because they accumulate (even without vesting), and ASC 710-10-25-1 requires accrual when rights either vest or accumulate, payment is probable, and the amount is estimable — the personal day liability ($70,000 = 280 x $250) reflects the probable cost of future absences that employees have earned but not yet taken", "Neither category requires accrual because compensated absences are recognized only when the time is actually taken", "Both categories are accrued at 50% of face value because unused days may not all be used"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): ASC 710-10-25-1 establishes four conditions for accruing compensated absences: (1) the obligation relates to services already rendered, (2) rights vest OR accumulate, (3) payment is probable, and (4) the amount is estimable. The critical word is "or" — the standard does not require both vesting and accumulation. Vacation days satisfy all four conditions: they vest (cash payout upon termination) and accumulate (carry forward). The accrual is 1,800 days x $250 = $450,000. Personal days also satisfy the conditions: they accumulate (carry forward to next year), employees will probably use them (payment in the form of paid absences is probable), and the amount is estimable. The accrual is 280 days x $250 = $70,000. The measurement difference between the two categories lies in the nature of the obligation: vacation day liability will be settled either through usage or termination payout, while personal day liability will be settled only through usage (since there is no termination payout). For personal days, the probability assessment focuses on whether employees will take the accumulated days. Choice A is incorrect because accumulation alone satisfies the ASC 710 criterion. Choice C is incorrect because the accrual principle requires recognition when earned, not when taken. Choice D has no basis in ASC 710.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 5236;

-- ID 5244: Payables — Short-term obligation reclassification analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Bellmont Industries has a $300,000 note payable due on March 1, Year 2, classified on the December 31, Year 1 balance sheet. On February 15, Year 2, Bellmont signs a 5-year noncancelable revolving credit agreement with a creditworthy bank and uses it to refinance the note before the financial statements are issued on March 10. Meanwhile, rival company Ashford Corp. has an identical $300,000 note due March 1, Year 2, and its CEO sends a letter of intent to a bank requesting refinancing, but no binding agreement exists at the financial statement issuance date. Compare how ASC 470-10 treats these two companies'' classification of the $300,000 note at December 31, Year 1.',
  choices = '["Both companies may classify the note as noncurrent because intent to refinance is the only requirement under ASC 470-10", "Bellmont may reclassify its note as noncurrent because it demonstrated both intent and ability — the executed noncancelable credit agreement with a capable lender constitutes sufficient evidence of ability to refinance on a long-term basis — Ashford must classify its note as current because a letter of intent without a binding agreement does not satisfy the ability requirement, leaving the $300,000 due within 12 months without demonstrated long-term refinancing capacity", "Ashford may reclassify but Bellmont cannot because only letters of intent qualify under ASC 470-10''s refinancing provisions", "Neither company may reclassify because short-term notes can never be presented as noncurrent regardless of refinancing arrangements"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): ASC 470-10-45-14 allows reclassification of a short-term obligation as noncurrent only when the entity demonstrates both the intent and the ability to refinance on a long-term basis. Ability is demonstrated by either: (a) actually issuing long-term debt or equity after the balance sheet date but before financial statement issuance, or (b) entering into a noncancelable financing agreement with a capable lender. Bellmont satisfies both: the February 15 execution of a 5-year noncancelable revolving credit agreement with a creditworthy bank demonstrates ability, and using it to refinance demonstrates intent. Ashford fails the ability test: a letter of intent is not a binding agreement — the bank has no obligation to lend, and Ashford''s refinancing remains contingent on successful negotiation. This distinction protects financial statement users from misleading reclassifications based on aspirational plans rather than committed financing. Choice A is incorrect because intent alone is insufficient — ability must also be demonstrated. Choice C reverses the analysis. Choice D is incorrect because ASC 470-10 specifically permits reclassification when both conditions are met.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 5244;

COMMIT;
